---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Header } from '../components/layout/Header';
import Footer from '../components/layout/Footer.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — SecurePass"
  description="How SecurePass generates passwords: Web Crypto randomness, rejection sampling, and fully local, auditable code."
  canonicalPath="/about"
>
  <Header client:load />
  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 sm:text-3xl dark:text-zinc-50">About SecurePass</h1>
    <div class="prose-securepass mt-6 space-y-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-600 sm:text-base dark:text-zinc-400">
      <p>
        SecurePass is a password generator that runs entirely in your browser. There is no
        account to create, no server that sees your passwords, and no analytics watching
        what you generate. The page you are reading <em>is</em> the whole application.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-50">How generation works</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          Randomness comes from the <strong>Web Crypto API</strong>
          (<code class="font-mono text-[0.9em]">crypto.getRandomValues()</code>) — the same
          cryptographic source browsers use for TLS keys. <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em]">Math.random()</code>
          is never used for anything secret.
        </li>
        <li>
          Random values are mapped to characters with <strong>rejection sampling</strong>,
          so every character in a pool is equally likely (no modulo bias).
        </li>
        <li>
          When “require one character from each group” is on, the forced characters are
          <strong> shuffled into random positions</strong> with a cryptographic shuffle —
          they never sit in predictable spots.
        </li>
        <li>
          Passphrases draw from the bundled <strong>EFF large word list</strong> (7,776 words,
          ~12.9 bits of entropy per word), also via the Web Crypto API.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-50">What the strength meter means</h2>
      <p>
        The meter shows an <strong>estimated entropy</strong> in bits, computed from the
        password length and the effective character pool (or word-list size for passphrases).
        It is a way to compare settings — not a guarantee. Real-world security also depends
        on phishing resistance, password reuse, and whether a password has leaked before.
        SecurePass does not claim to make you “unhackable”; a password manager plus unique
        passwords per site remains the best practical advice.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-50">Technology</h2>
      <p>
        Built with <strong>Astro</strong> (static-first pages), <strong>React</strong> islands
        (only the interactive generator hydrates), <strong>TypeScript</strong>,
        <strong> Tailwind CSS</strong>, and <strong>Lucide</strong> icons. The generation logic
        is deliberately separated from the UI in <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em]">src/lib/</code>
        so it stays small, testable, and easy to audit.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-50">Open source</h2>
      <p>
        The full source is available on{' '}
        <a
          href="https://github.com/cloudleapasxm/password-generator"
          class="font-medium text-blue-700 underline decoration-blue-700/30 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 dark:decoration-blue-400/30 dark:hover:decoration-blue-400"
        >
          GitHub
        </a>
        . If you can read code, you can verify every claim on this page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
